{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-008", "category": "reasoning", "title": "SLA deadline over the holidays", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 3510, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 209, "reasoning_tokens": 78, "input_tokens": 121, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 313, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "2026-12-30 12:30", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "2026-12-30 12:30"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjba84bbb4-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb6875e24-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:30-0500", "answer": "Count only hours when the office is open:\n\n- **December 24:** 16:30\u201317:00 = 0.5 hours\n- **December 25\u201327:** Holiday and weekend = 0 hours\n- **December 28:** 09:00\u201317:00 = 8 hours\n- **December 29:** 09:00\u201317:00 = 8 hours\n- **December 30:** The remaining 3.5 hours run from 09:00 to 12:30.\n\nANSWER: 2026-12-30 12:30"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-004", "category": "reasoning", "title": "New York to London across the DST gap", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 8174, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 473, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 103, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 1354, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjb7c77019-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb686e8f1-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:30-0500", "answer": "I need to find the London time for a 09:30 New York meeting on specific Mondays in March 2026.\n\nFirst, let me determine the time zone offsets. New York is in Eastern Time (ET), and London is in GMT/UTC (or BST during daylight saving).\n\n**Key dates for daylight saving in 2026:**\n- US DST starts: Second Sunday in March = March 8, 2026\n- UK BST starts: Last Sunday in March = March 29, 2026\n\nSo for these dates:\n- **March 2, 2026**: Before US DST, before UK BST. New York = EST (UTC-5), London = GMT (UTC+0). Difference: 5 hours.\n- **March 9, 2026**: After US DST (started March 8), before UK BST. New York = EDT (UTC-4), London = GMT (UTC+0). Difference: 4 hours.\n- **March 16, 2026**: After US DST, before UK BST. New York = EDT (UTC-4), London = GMT (UTC+0). Difference: 4 hours.\n- **March 23, 2026**: After US DST, before UK BST. New York = EDT (UTC-4), London = GMT (UTC+0). Difference: 4 hours.\n- **March 30, 2026**: After US DST, after UK BST (started March 29). New York = EDT (UTC-4), London = BST (UTC+1). Difference: 5 hours.\n\nNow calculating London times (09:30 New York + offset):\n\n- **March 2**: 09:30 + 5 hours = **14:30**\n- **March 9**: 09:30 + 4 hours = **13:30**\n- **March 16**: 09:30 + 4 hours = **13:30**\n- **March 23**: 09:30 + 4 hours = **13:30**\n- **March 30**: 09:30 + 5 hours = **14:30**\n\nANSWER: 14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-001", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Six-digit numbers with digit sum 27", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 10189, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1621, "reasoning_tokens": 1360, "input_tokens": 44, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 729, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 4149, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "55252", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "55252"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjb6c89d9f-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb686e8f0-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:31-0500", "answer": "Pad each number to 6 digits with leading zeros. Then we need the number of solutions to\n\n\\[\nd_1+d_2+\\cdots+d_6=27,\\qquad 0\\le d_i\\le 9.\n\\]\n\nWithout the upper bound, the number of nonnegative solutions is\n\n\\[\n\\binom{27+6-1}{6-1}=\\binom{32}{5}=201376.\n\\]\n\nSubtract cases where some digit is at least 10. For one such digit, set it minus 10, leaving sum 17 among 6 digits:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{17+6-1}{5}=\\binom{22}{5}=26334.\n\\]\n\nThere are 6 choices, so subtract \\(6\\cdot 26334\\).\n\nAdd back cases where two digits are at least 10, leaving sum 7 among 6 digits:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{7+6-1}{5}=\\binom{12}{5}=792.\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) such pairs, so add \\(15\\cdot 792\\).\n\nThus the count is\n\n\\[\n201376-6(26334)+15(792)=55252.\n\\]\n\nANSWER: 55252"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-007", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Friday the 13ths in 2026-2030", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_low", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning low)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 6773, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 316, "reasoning_tokens": 186, "input_tokens": 55, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 322, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "9", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "9"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjb9377b1d-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb6875e25-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:31-0500", "answer": "A month has a Friday the 13th exactly when it begins on a Sunday.\n\n| Year | Months with Friday the 13th | Count |\n|---|---|---:|\n| 2026 | February, March, November | 3 |\n| 2027 | August | 1 |\n| 2028 | October | 1 |\n| 2029 | April, July | 2 |\n| 2030 | September, December | 2 |\n\nTotal: \\(3 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 9\\).\n\nANSWER: 9"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-002", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Seven-seat row logic puzzle", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 9094, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1558, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 176, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 4039, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjb7d9236b-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb686e8ef-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:31-0500", "answer": "I need to find the seating order from seat 1 to seat 7.\n\nLet me list the clues:\n1. Exactly two people sit between Ben and Dev (so |Ben - Dev| = 3 positions)\n2. Eli and Gus do not sit next to each other\n3. Dev sits somewhere to the right of Fay (Dev > Fay)\n4. Fay sits somewhere to the right of Cai (Fay > Cai)\n5. Gus sits somewhere to the right of Eli (Gus > Eli)\n6. Exactly two people sit between Ana and Eli (so |Ana - Eli| = 3 positions)\n7. Ana sits immediately to the left of Fay (Ana = Fay - 1, so Ana and Fay are adjacent with Ana on left)\n\nFrom clue 7: Ana and Fay are adjacent, with Ana immediately left of Fay. So we have the block [Ana, Fay].\n\nFrom clue 4: Cai < Fay, and from clue 3: Fay < Dev. So Cai < Fay < Dev.\n\nFrom clue 7 and 4: Cai < Ana < Fay (since Ana is immediately left of Fay, and Cai is somewhere left of Fay).\n\nActually, let me be more careful. Cai < Fay, and Ana = Fay - 1. So Cai could be anywhere left of Fay, including possibly left of Ana or between Ana and Fay... but Ana is immediately left of Fay, so there's no seat between them. So Cai < Ana or Cai = Ana (impossible, different people). So Cai < Ana.\n\nSo we have: Cai < Ana < Fay < Dev.\n\nFrom clue 6: |Ana - Eli| = 3. So Eli is either Ana + 3 or Ana - 3.\n\nFrom clue 5: Gus > Eli.\n\nFrom clue 1: |Ben - Dev| = 3.\n\nLet me try to place the block [Ana, Fay]. Since Cai < Ana and Fay < Dev, and we need room for everyone.\n\nLet me denote positions 1-7.\n\nSince Cai < Ana < Fay < Dev, we need at least 4 positions for these four people in order.\n\nLet me try Ana in position 2: then Fay is in 3. Cai must be in 1. Then Dev > 3, so Dev in 4, 5, 6, or 7.\n\nIf Ana=2, Fay=3, Cai=1. Then from clue 6, |2 - Eli| = 3, so Eli = 5 (since Eli = -1 is impossible).\n\nSo Eli = 5. Then Gus > 5, so Gus in 6 or 7.\n\nDev > 3, and |Ben - Dev| = 3.\n\nRemaining positions: 4, 6, 7 (and we need to place Ben, Dev, Gus).\n\nWait, let me check: positions used are 1(Cai), 2(Ana), 3(Fay), 5(Eli). Remaining: 4, 6, 7 for Ben, Dev, Gus.\n\nDev > 3, so Dev in 4, 6, or 7.\n\nIf Dev = 4: |Ben - 4| = 3, so Ben = 1 or 7. But 1 is Cai, so Ben = 7. Then Gus = 6. Check: Gus > Eli? 6 > 5, yes. Check Eli and Gus not adjacent? Eli=5, Gus=6, they ARE adjacent! Violates clue 2.\n\nIf Dev = 6: |Ben - 6| = 3, so Ben = 3 or 9. 3 is Fay, 9 invalid. No good.\n\nIf Dev = 7: |Ben - 7| = 3, so Ben = 4. Then Gus = 6. Check: Gus > Eli? 6 > 5, yes. Check Eli and Gus not adjacent? Eli=5, Gus=6, they ARE adjacent! Violates clue 2.\n\nSo Ana = 2 doesn't work.\n\nLet me try Ana = 3: then Fay = 4. Cai < 3, so Cai in 1 or 2.\n\nFrom clue 6: |3 - Eli| = 3, so Eli = 6 (or 0, invalid).\n\nSo Eli = 6. Then Gus > 6, so Gus = 7.\n\nCai < Ana = 3, so Cai in 1 or 2. Fay = 4 < Dev, so Dev in 5, 6, 7. But 6 is Eli, 7 is Gus, so Dev = 5.\n\nCheck: |Ben - Dev| = |Ben - 5| = 3, so Ben = 2 or 8. So Ben = 2.\n\nThen Cai = 1 (only position left).\n\nLet me verify: Cai=1, Ben=2, Ana=3, Fay=4, Dev=5, Eli=6, Gus=7.\n\nCheck all clues:\n1. |Ben - Dev| = |2-5| = 3 \u2713 (exactly two people between: Ana, Fay)\n2. Eli=6, Gus=7, they ARE adjacent! \u2717\n\nFails clue 2.\n\nLet me try Ana = 4: then Fay = 5. Cai < 4.\n\nFrom clue 6: |4 - Eli| = 3, so Eli = 1 or 7.\n\nCase Eli = 1: Then Gus > 1. Fay = 5 < Dev, so Dev in 6 or 7.\n\nPositions used: 1(Eli), 4(Ana), 5(Fay). Remaining: 2, 3, 6, 7 for Cai, Ben, Dev, Gus.\n\nCai < 4, so Cai in 2 or 3.\n\nDev > 5, so Dev in 6 or 7. |Ben - Dev| = 3.\n\nIf Dev = 6: Ben = 3 or 9. So Ben = 3. Then Cai = 2, Gus = 7. Check Gus > Eli: 7 > 1 \u2713. Check Eli, Gus not adjacent: 1 and 7, not adjacent \u2713.\n\nCheck: Cai=2, Ben=3, Ana=4, Fay=5, Dev=6, Eli=1, Gus=7.\n\nWait, let me reorder by position: 1(Eli), 2(Cai), 3(Ben), 4(Ana), 5(Fay), 6(Dev), 7(Gus).\n\nCheck all clues:\n1. |Ben - Dev| = |3-6| = 3 \u2713 (two people between: Ana, Fay)\n2. Eli=1, Gus=7, not adjacent \u2713\n3. Dev=6 > Fay=5 \u2713\n4. Fay=5 > Cai=2 \u2713\n5. Gus=7 > Eli=1 \u2713\n6. |Ana - Eli| = |4-1| = 3 \u2713 (two people between: Cai, Ben)\n7. Ana=4 immediately left of Fay=5 \u2713\n\nAll clues satisfied!\n\nLet me verify there are no other solutions, but this works.\n\nAN"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-008", "category": "reasoning", "title": "SLA deadline over the holidays", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": false, "score": 0.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 5609, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 542, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 137, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 1399, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 0.0, "pass": false, "expected": "2026-12-30 12:30", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "2026-12-29 12:30"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjba84bbb5-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb6875e24-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:32-0500", "answer": "Let's calculate the resolution deadline step by step.\n\n### Given:\n- **Ticket opened:** Thursday, December 24, 2026 at 16:30\n- **Resolution deadline:** 20 business hours later\n- **Business hours:** Monday to Friday, 09:00 to 17:00 (8 hours per day)\n- **Holidays:** December 25, 2026 (Friday) and January 1, 2027 (Friday) (no business hours)\n\n### Step 1: Calculate remaining business hours on December 24, 2026 (Thursday)\n- Business hours on December 24: 09:00 to 17:00 (8 hours)\n- Ticket opened at 16:30, so remaining business hours on December 24:\n  - 17:00 - 16:30 = 0.5 hours\n\n### Step 2: Subtract remaining hours from the deadline\n- Remaining business hours to account for: 20 - 0.5 = 19.5 hours\n\n### Step 3: Move to the next business day (December 28, 2026, Monday)\n- December 25 (Friday) is a holiday, so the next business day is December 28 (Monday).\n- Business hours on December 28: 09:00 to 17:00 (8 hours)\n- Subtract 8 hours from the remaining 19.5 hours: 19.5 - 8 = 11.5 hours\n\n### Step 4: Move to the next business day (December 29, 2026, Tuesday)\n- Business hours on December 29: 09:00 to 17:00 (8 hours)\n- Subtract 8 hours from the remaining 11.5 hours: 11.5 - 8 = 3.5 hours\n\n### Step 5: Calculate the deadline on December 29, 2026\n- 3.5 business hours after 09:00 on December 29:\n  - 09:00 + 3.5 hours = 12:30\n\n### Final Deadline:\n- December 29, 2026 at 12:30\n\nANSWER: 2026-12-29 12:30"}
