{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-007", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Friday the 13ths in 2026-2030", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 10568, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1220, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 62, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 2588, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "9", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "9"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjb9377b1f-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb6875e25-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:35-0500", "answer": "To determine how many times Friday the 13th occurs from January 1, 2026 through December 31, 2030, we can analyze each year in the given range. Here's the step-by-step breakdown:\n\n### Key Observations:\n1. A month has a Friday the 13th if the 13th day of the month falls on a Friday.\n2. The day of the week for the 13th can be calculated using Zeller's Congruence or by leveraging known algorithms for the Gregorian calendar.\n3. Alternatively, we can use a perpetual calendar or programming to count the occurrences.\n\n### Year-by-Year Analysis:\nWe will check each month in the years 2026 to 2030 for a Friday the 13th.\n\n#### 2026:\n- January 13, 2026: Tuesday\n- February 13, 2026: Friday \u2705\n- March 13, 2026: Friday \u2705\n- April 13, 2026: Monday\n- May 13, 2026: Wednesday\n- June 13, 2026: Saturday\n- July 13, 2026: Monday\n- August 13, 2026: Thursday\n- September 13, 2026: Sunday\n- October 13, 2026: Tuesday\n- November 13, 2026: Friday \u2705\n- December 13, 2026: Sunday\n**Total for 2026: 3**\n\n#### 2027:\n- January 13, 2027: Wednesday\n- February 13, 2027: Saturday\n- March 13, 2027: Saturday\n- April 13, 2027: Tuesday\n- May 13, 2027: Thursday\n- June 13, 2027: Sunday\n- July 13, 2027: Tuesday\n- August 13, 2027: Friday \u2705\n- September 13, 2027: Monday\n- October 13, 2027: Wednesday\n- November 13, 2027: Saturday\n- December 13, 2027: Monday\n**Total for 2027: 1**\n\n#### 2028:\n- January 13, 2028: Thursday\n- February 13, 2028: Sunday\n- March 13, 2028: Monday\n- April 13, 2028: Wednesday\n- May 13, 2028: Saturday\n- June 13, 2028: Tuesday\n- July 13, 2028: Thursday\n- August 13, 2028: Sunday\n- September 13, 2028: Wednesday\n- October 13, 2028: Friday \u2705\n- November 13, 2028: Monday\n- December 13, 2028: Wednesday\n**Total for 2028: 1**\n\n#### 2029:\n- January 13, 2029: Saturday\n- February 13, 2029: Tuesday\n- March 13, 2029: Tuesday\n- April 13, 2029: Friday \u2705\n- May 13, 2029: Sunday\n- June 13, 2029: Wednesday\n- July 13, 2029: Friday \u2705\n- August 13, 2029: Monday\n- September 13, 2029: Thursday\n- October 13, 2029: Saturday\n- November 13, 2029: Tuesday\n- December 13, 2029: Thursday\n**Total for 2029: 2**\n\n#### 2030:\n- January 13, 2030: Sunday\n- February 13, 2030: Wednesday\n- March 13, 2030: Wednesday\n- April 13, 2030: Saturday\n- May 13, 2030: Monday\n- June 13, 2030: Thursday\n- July 13, 2030: Saturday\n- August 13, 2030: Tuesday\n- September 13, 2030: Friday \u2705\n- October 13, 2030: Sunday\n- November 13, 2030: Wednesday\n- December 13, 2030: Friday \u2705\n**Total for 2030: 2**\n\n### Summing Up:\n- 2026: 3\n- 2027: 1\n- 2028: 1\n- 2029: 2\n- 2030: 2\n\n**Total occurrences from 2026 to 2030: 3 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 9**\n\nANSWER: 9"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-002", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Seven-seat row logic puzzle", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 13353, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 634, "reasoning_tokens": 512, "input_tokens": 172, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 374, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjb7d9236d-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb686e8ef-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:36-0500", "answer": "The seating order satisfies every clue:\n- Ben (3) and Dev (6) have exactly two people between them.\n- Eli (1) and Gus (7) are not adjacent, and Gus is to Eli\u2019s right.\n- Cai (2) is left of Fay (5), who is left of Dev (6).\n- Ana (4) and Eli (1) have exactly two people between them.\n- Ana (4) sits immediately to the left of Fay (5).\n\nANSWER: Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-005", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Divisible by exactly two of 4, 6 and 9", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 15474, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1883, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 60, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 4728, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "83334", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "83334"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjb6b67521-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb6871002-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:36-0500", "answer": "Alright, I have a problem to solve:\n\n**How many integers n with 1 <= n <= 1,000,000 are divisible by exactly two of the three numbers 4, 6, and 9?**\n\nFirst, I need to understand what's being asked. I need to find all integers from 1 to 1,000,000 that are divisible by exactly two out of the three numbers: 4, 6, and 9.\n\n### Understanding the Problem\nI need numbers divisible by exactly two of these three numbers. That means:\n1. Divisible by 4 and 6, but not 9.\n2. Divisible by 4 and 9, but not 6.\n3. Divisible by 6 and 9, but not 4.\n\n### Approach\nI think I need to find the count of numbers divisible by pairs of these numbers but not the third one. To do this, I recall the principle of inclusion and exclusion.\n\n### Step 1: Find Numbers Divisible by Pairs\nFirst, I need to find numbers divisible by any two of the numbers.\n\n#### 1. Divisible by 4 and 6\nA number divisible by both 4 and 6 must be divisible by the least common multiple (LCM) of 4 and 6.\n\n- LCM of 4 and 6:\n  - Prime factors of 4: 2^2\n  - Prime factors of 6: 2 * 3\n  - LCM is the highest power of each prime: 2^2 * 3 = 12\n\nSo, numbers divisible by both 4 and 6 are divisible by 12.\n\nBut I need numbers divisible by 4 and 6 but not by 9.\n\n- Total numbers divisible by 12 up to 1,000,000: floor(1,000,000 / 12) = 83,333\n- But I need to exclude those divisible by 9 as well.\n\nNumbers divisible by both 12 and 9 must be divisible by the LCM of 12 and 9.\n\n- LCM of 12 and 9:\n  - Prime factors of 12: 2^2 * 3\n  - Prime factors of 9: 3^2\n  - LCM: 2^2 * 3^2 = 36\n\n- Total numbers divisible by 36 up to 1,000,000: floor(1,000,000 / 36) \u2248 27,777\n\n- Therefore, numbers divisible by 12 but not by 9: 83,333 - 27,777 = 55,556\n\n#### 2. Divisible by 4 and 9\nNow, numbers divisible by both 4 and 9.\n\n- LCM of 4 and 9:\n  - Prime factors of 4: 2^2\n  - Prime factors of 9: 3^2\n  - LCM: 2^2 * 3^2 = 36\n\n- Total numbers divisible by 36 up to 1,000,000: floor(1,000,000 / 36) \u2248 27,777\n\nBut I need numbers divisible by 4 and 9 but not by 6.\n\n- Numbers divisible by 36 and 6 must be divisible by the LCM of 36 and 6.\n  - LCM of 36 and 6:\n    - 36 is already divisible by 6, so LCM is 36.\n\n- Therefore, all numbers divisible by 36 are divisible by 6.\n\nBut the condition is numbers divisible by 4 and 9 but not by 6. However, any number divisible by both 4 and 9 is divisible by 36, and 36 is divisible by 6. Thus, any number divisible by both 4 and 9 is also divisible by 6.\n\n- Therefore, the count is zero.\n\n#### 3. Divisible by 6 and 9\nNow, numbers divisible by both 6 and 9.\n\n- LCM of 6 and 9:\n  - Prime factors of 6: 2 * 3\n  - Prime factors of 9: 3^2\n  - LCM: 2 * 3^2 = 18\n\n- Total numbers divisible by 18 up to 1,000,000: floor(1,000,000 / 18) \u2248 55,555\n\nBut I need numbers divisible by 6 and 9 but not by 4.\n\n- Numbers divisible by both 18 and 4 must be divisible by the LCM of 18 and 4.\n  - LCM of 18 and 4:\n    - Prime factors of 18: 2 * 3^2\n    - Prime factors of 4: 2^2\n    - LCM: 2^2 * 3^2 = 36\n\n- Total numbers divisible by 36 up to 1,000,000: floor(1,000,000 / 36) \u2248 27,777\n\n- Therefore, numbers divisible by 18 but not by 4: 55,555 - 27,777 = 27,778\n\n### Verification\nI need to ensure that I'm not double-counting or missing anything.\n\n- **First case**: Numbers divisible by 4 and 6 but not 9 \u2192 55,556\n- **Second case**: Numbers divisible by 4 and 9 but not 6 \u2192 0 (since any number divisible by both 4 and 9 is divisible by 6)\n- **Third case**: Numbers divisible by 6 and 9 but not 4 \u2192 27,778\n\n### Total Count\nNow, I add the counts from the valid cases:\n- 55,556 (from 4 and 6 but not 9)\n- 0 (from 4 and 9 but not 6)\n- 27,778 (from 6 and 9 but not 4)\n\nTotal = 55,556 + 0 + 27,778 = 83,334\n\n### Cross-Checking\nI wonder if I need to consider numbers divisible by all three. But the problem asks for numbers divisible by exactly two. So, numbers divisible by all three are excluded, which is already handled in the above cases.\n\n### Final Calculation\nJust to be sure, I can think about the exact values:\n\n1. **Divisible by 4 and 6 (i.e., 12) but n"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-003", "category": "reasoning", "title": "String rotate-and-shift trace", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_low", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning low)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 7389, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 484, "reasoning_tokens": 300, "input_tokens": 124, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 427, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "kywasurrz", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "kywasurrz"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjbb84c6ab-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb686e8ee-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:36-0500", "answer": "Each iteration first rearranges the string, then shifts the letters at even indices (`0, 2, 4, 6, 8`) forward by `i`, wrapping around the alphabet.\n\n| `i` | After `s[i:] + s[:i][::-1]` | After shifting even-indexed letters |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `ondutcorc` | `pneudtprd` |\n| 2 | `eudtprdnp` | `guftrrfnr` |\n| 3 | `trrfnrfug` | `wrufqriuj` |\n| 4 | `qriujfurw` | `urmunfyra` |\n| 5 | `fyranumru` | `kywasurrz` |\n\nANSWER: kywasurrz"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-001", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Six-digit numbers with digit sum 27", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 17097, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1837, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 50, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 5383, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "55252", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "55252"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjb6c89da2-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb686e8f0-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:38-0500", "answer": "Alright, I have a problem to solve:\n\n**How many integers n with 1 \u2264 n \u2264 999,999 have a decimal digit sum of exactly 27?**\n\n### Understanding the Problem\nFirst, I need to understand the range and the condition:\n- **Range**: n can be from 1 to 999,999. That means numbers from 1-digit up to 6-digit numbers.\n- **Condition**: The sum of the digits of n must be exactly 27.\n\n### Approach\nI recall that problems like this are related to combinatorics, specifically counting the number of solutions to an equation under certain constraints. The digit sum condition can be translated into a problem of finding the number of combinations of digits (0-9) that add up to 27, but with the constraint that the number has up to 6 digits.\n\n### Step 1: Understand the Maximum Digit Sum\nFirst, I need to find the maximum possible digit sum for a 6-digit number. The largest 6-digit number is 999,999, and its digit sum is 9 * 6 = 54.\n\nSince 27 is within the range of possible sums (from 1 to 54), it's feasible.\n\n### Step 2: Formulate the Problem\nI need to find the number of 6-digit sequences (d1, d2, ..., d6) where:\n- 0 \u2264 di \u2264 9 for each i (since digits range from 0 to 9).\n- d1 + d2 + ... + d6 = 27.\n- However, n can be from 1 to 999,999, so numbers with fewer than 6 digits are equivalent to 6-digit numbers with leading zeros. For example, 123 is equivalent to 000123.\n\nBut, n must be at least 1, so the case where all digits are zero (000000) is excluded.\n\n### Step 3: Use Stars and Bars\nThe problem is similar to finding the number of non-negative integer solutions to the equation:\nd1 + d2 + ... + d6 = 27, with 0 \u2264 di \u2264 9.\n\nBut, the standard stars and bars theorem gives the number of non-negative solutions without the upper bound constraint. The formula is C(n + k - 1, k - 1), where n is the total sum and k is the number of variables.\n\nFor this problem:\n- n = 27 (sum)\n- k = 6 (digits)\n\nTotal solutions without constraints: C(27 + 6 - 1, 6 - 1) = C(32, 5).\n\nBut, we have the constraint di \u2264 9. So, we need to subtract the cases where one or more digits are \u2265 10.\n\n### Step 4: Apply Inclusion-Exclusion Principle\nFirst, calculate the total number of solutions without any constraints: C(32, 5).\n\nNow, subtract the cases where at least one digit is \u2265 10.\n\nFor one digit \u2265 10, say d1 \u2265 10. Let d1' = d1 - 10, then d1' \u2265 0.\nThe equation becomes d1' + d2 + ... + d6 = 27 - 10 = 17.\n\nNumber of solutions: C(17 + 6 - 1, 6 - 1) = C(22, 5).\n\nSince any of the 6 digits can be the one \u2265 10, multiply by 6: 6 * C(22, 5).\n\nBut, we might have double-counted cases where two digits are \u2265 10. So, add those back.\n\nFor two digits \u2265 10, say d1, d2 \u2265 10. Let d1' = d1 - 10, d2' = d2 - 10.\nThe equation becomes d1' + d2' + d3 + ... + d6 = 27 - 20 = 7.\n\nNumber of solutions: C(7 + 6 - 1, 6 - 1) = C(12, 5).\n\nThere are C(6, 2) pairs of digits, so total: C(6, 2) * C(12, 5).\n\nFor three digits \u2265 10, the sum becomes 27 - 30 = -3, which is negative. No solutions here.\n\nThus, the inclusion-exclusion stops here.\n\n### Final Count\nTotal number of solutions:\nC(32, 5) - 6 * C(22, 5) + C(6, 2) * C(12, 5).\n\nBut, we must exclude the case where all digits are zero (000000), but since the sum is 27, it's already excluded.\n\n### Verification\nLet's compute the values:\n1. C(32, 5) = 201376\n2. C(22, 5) = 26334 \u2192 6 * C(22, 5) = 158004\n3. C(12, 5) = 792 \u2192 C(6, 2) = 15 \u2192 15 * C(12, 5) = 11880\n\nNow, plug into the formula:\n201376 - 158004 + 11880 = 201376 - 158004 = 43372 + 11880 = 55252\n\n### Check for Numbers with Fewer Digits\nThe above calculation includes numbers with leading zeros, which correspond to numbers with fewer than 6 digits. For example, 000123 is equivalent to 123.\n\nBut, the problem specifies n from 1 to 999,999, so numbers like 000000 are excluded, but 000000 has a digit sum of 0, not 27. Therefore, all numbers in our count are valid since they have a digit sum of 27 and correspond to numbers from 1 to 999,999.\n\n### Edge Cases\n- **Single-digit numbers**: The maximum sum is 9. 27 > 9 \u2192 No single-digit numbers.\n-"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-006", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Four dice: max is twice the min", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 13919, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1374, "reasoning_tokens": 1078, "input_tokens": 61, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 653, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 3007, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "29/216", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "29/216"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjb8f8c456-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb6873713-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:38-0500", "answer": "The total number of outcomes is \\(6^4 = 1296\\).\n\nLet the smallest value be \\(m\\). Then the largest must be \\(2m\\), so possible \\((m, 2m)\\) pairs are \\((1,2), (2,4), (3,6)\\).\n\n- For \\((1,2)\\): all dice show 1 or 2, with at least one 1 and one 2:  \n  \\[\n  2^4 - 2 = 14\n  \\]\n- For \\((2,4)\\): all dice show 2, 3, or 4, with at least one 2 and one 4:  \n  \\[\n  3^4 - 2\\cdot 2^4 + 1 = 81 - 32 + 1 = 50\n  \\]\n- For \\((3,6)\\): all dice show 3, 4, 5, or 6, with at least one 3 and one 6:  \n  \\[\n  4^4 - 2\\cdot 3^4 + 2^4 = 256 - 162 + 16 = 110\n  \\]\n\nFavorable outcomes:\n\\[\n14 + 50 + 110 = 174\n\\]\n\nProbability:\n\\[\n\\frac{174}{1296} = \\frac{29}{216}\n\\]\n\nANSWER: 29/216"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-007", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Friday the 13ths in 2026-2030", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 13748, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 2043, "reasoning_tokens": 1948, "input_tokens": 55, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 204, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 5461, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "9", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "9"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjb9377b1b-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb6875e25-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:38-0500", "answer": "The Friday the 13ths are:\n\n- 2026: February, March, November \u2014 3\n- 2027: August \u2014 1\n- 2028: October \u2014 1\n- 2029: April, July \u2014 2\n- 2030: September, December \u2014 2\n\nTotal: \\(3 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 9\\)\n\nANSWER: 9"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-003", "category": "reasoning", "title": "String rotate-and-shift trace", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 10200, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 836, "reasoning_tokens": 653, "input_tokens": 124, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 434, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "kywasurrz", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "kywasurrz"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjbb849f9a-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb686e8ee-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:39-0500", "answer": "Each iteration first moves the reversed prefix of length `i` to the end, then shifts letters at zero-based even positions (`0, 2, 4, 6, 8`) forward by `i`, wrapping around the alphabet.\n\n| `i` | After rearranging | After shifting |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `onductorc` | `pneudtprd` |\n| 2 | `eudtprdnp` | `guftrrfnr` |\n| 3 | `trrfnrfug` | `wrufqriuj` |\n| 4 | `qriujfurw` | `urmunfyra` |\n| 5 | `fyranumru` | `kywasurrz` |\n\nANSWER: kywasurrz"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-004", "category": "reasoning", "title": "New York to London across the DST gap", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 16640, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 2779, "reasoning_tokens": 2664, "input_tokens": 103, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 296, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 8207, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjb7c7701a-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb686e8f1-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:39-0500", "answer": "DST transitions in 2026: New York switches to EDT on Sunday March 8; London switches to BST on Sunday March 29.\n\n- March 2: New York EST, London GMT \u2192 14:30\n- March 9, 16, 23: New York EDT, London GMT \u2192 13:30\n- March 30: New York EDT, London BST \u2192 14:30\n\nANSWER: 14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-006", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Four dice: max is twice the min", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 18981, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 2079, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 64, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 6539, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "29/216", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "29/216"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjb8f8c458-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb6873713-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:43-0500", "answer": "Alright, I have a probability problem to solve:\n\n**Problem Statement:**\nFour fair six-sided dice are rolled. What is the probability that the largest value showing is exactly twice the smallest value showing? Give the probability as a fraction in lowest terms.\n\n### Understanding the Problem\nFirst, I need to understand what's being asked. We're rolling four standard six-sided dice (each die has faces numbered from 1 to 6). I need to find the probability that the largest number rolled is exactly twice the smallest number rolled.\n\n### Total Possible Outcomes\nSince each die is independent and has 6 faces, the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four dice is:\n6 \u00d7 6 \u00d7 6 \u00d7 6 = 6^4 = 1296.\n\n### Favorable Outcomes\nI need to find the number of outcomes where the largest number is exactly twice the smallest number.\n\n#### Step 1: Identify Possible Pairs (Min, Max)\nThe condition is max = 2 \u00d7 min. Since the dice are six-sided, the possible pairs (min, max) must satisfy:\n1. min must be an integer from 1 to 6.\n2. max must be an integer from 1 to 6.\n3. max = 2 \u00d7 min.\n\nLet's list the possible pairs:\n- If min = 1, max = 2 \u00d7 1 = 2.\n- If min = 2, max = 4.\n- If min = 3, max = 6.\n\nFor min = 4, max = 8, but 8 is beyond the range of a six-sided die. So, no valid pairs beyond min = 3.\n\nThus, the valid (min, max) pairs are:\n1. (1, 2)\n2. (2, 4)\n3. (3, 6)\n\n#### Step 2: Count Outcomes for Each Pair\nFor each pair (min, max), I need to count the number of ways four dice can show numbers such that:\n- The smallest number is min.\n- The largest number is max.\n- All numbers are between min and max, inclusive.\n\n##### Case 1: (1, 2)\n- All four dice must show numbers between 1 and 2.\n- The smallest number must be 1.\n- The largest number must be 2.\n- At least one die must be 1 (to ensure min is 1).\n- At least one die must be 2 (to ensure max is 2).\n\nThis is similar to counting the number of ways to have four dice where:\n- All dice are 1 or 2.\n- Not all dice are 1 (since max must be 2).\n- Not all dice are 2 (since min must be 1).\n\nBut, more precisely, it's the number of outcomes where:\n- At least one die is 1.\n- At least one die is 2.\n- The rest can be either 1 or 2.\n\nThis is the total number of outcomes with 1s and 2s minus the outcomes with all 1s or all 2s.\n\nTotal outcomes with 1s and 2s: 2^4 = 16.\nOutcomes with all 1s: 1.\nOutcomes with all 2s: 1.\n\nBut, we need both at least one 1 and at least one 2. So, it's 16 - 1 - 1 = 14.\n\nBut, is this accurate? Yes, because:\n- Total combinations: 2^4 = 16.\n- Subtract the case where all are 1 (1 outcome).\n- Subtract the case where all are 2 (1 outcome).\n- The remaining 14 outcomes have at least one 1 and at least one 2.\n\n##### Case 2: (2, 4)\n- All four dice must show numbers between 2 and 4.\n- The smallest number must be 2.\n- The largest number must be 4.\n- At least one die must be 2.\n- At least one die must be 4.\n- The other dice can be 2, 3, or 4.\n\nThis is more complex. We need to count the number of outcomes where:\n- The minimum is exactly 2.\n- The maximum is exactly 4.\n\nFirst, find the total number of outcomes where all dice are between 2 and 4: 3^4 = 81.\n\nBut we need to ensure:\n1. At least one die is 2 (min condition).\n2. At least one die is 4 (max condition).\n3. No die is less than 2 (which is already satisfied since the range is 2-4).\n4. No die is greater than 4 (also satisfied).\n\nHowever, we must exclude cases where the minimum is greater than 2 or the maximum is less than 4.\n\nBut, it's easier to use the inclusion-exclusion principle.\n\nTotal outcomes: 3^4 = 81.\n\nSubtract outcomes where no die is 2 (i.e., all dice are 3 or 4): 2^4 = 16.\nSubtract outcomes where no die is 4 (i.e., all dice are 2 or 3): 2^4 = 16.\n\nBut now, we've double-subtracted the outcomes where no die is 2 and no die is 4 (i.e., all dice are 3). So, we need to add that back: 1^4 = 1.\n\nThus, the number of outcomes with at least one 2 and at least one 4 is:\n81 - 16 - 16 + 1 = 50.\n\nBut, we need to ensure that the minimum is exactly 2 and the max"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-002", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Seven-seat row logic puzzle", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 22274, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 2341, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 174, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 6555, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjb7d9236e-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjb686e8ef-d029-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:08:45-0500", "answer": "Alright, let's tackle this seating arrangement problem step by step. We have seven people: Ana, Ben, Cai, Dev, Eli, Fay, and Gus, who need to be seated in seats numbered 1 to 7 from left to right. We'll go through each clue one by one and see how they constrain the possible arrangements.\n\n### Clue 1: Exactly two people sit between Ben and Dev.\nThis means Ben and Dev are separated by two seats, so they could be in positions like:\n- Ben in 1, Dev in 4\n- Ben in 2, Dev in 5\n- Ben in 3, Dev in 6\n- Ben in 4, Dev in 7\nOr the other way around (Dev first, then Ben), but since the next clues will help us determine the order, we'll keep both possibilities in mind for now.\n\n### Clue 2: Eli and Gus do not sit next to each other.\nThis means there's at least one seat between Eli and Gus.\n\n### Clue 3: Dev sits somewhere to the right of Fay.\nThis means in the seating order, Fay comes before Dev.\n\n### Clue 4: Fay sits somewhere to the right of Cai.\nThis means Cai comes before Fay in the seating order.\n\n### Clue 5: Gus sits somewhere to the right of Eli.\nEli comes before Gus in the seating order.\n\n### Clue 6: Exactly two people sit between Ana and Eli.\nSimilar to the first clue, this means Ana and Eli are separated by two seats. So possible positions:\n- Ana in 1, Eli in 4\n- Ana in 2, Eli in 5\n- Ana in 3, Eli in 6\n- Ana in 4, Eli in 7\nOr Eli first, then Ana, but given that Gus is to the right of Eli (from Clue 5), it's more likely Ana is to the left of Eli.\n\n### Clue 7: Ana sits immediately to the left of Fay.\nThis means Ana and Fay are next to each other, with Ana in a lower-numbered seat than Fay. So if Ana is in seat X, Fay is in seat X+1.\n\nNow, let's try to combine these clues to find a consistent arrangement.\n\nFrom Clue 7: Ana and Fay are adjacent, with Ana left of Fay.\nFrom Clue 6: Exactly two people between Ana and Eli. So if Ana is in X, Eli is in X+3.\n\nPossible positions for Ana and Fay:\n- Ana in 1, Fay in 2\n- Ana in 2, Fay in 3\n- Ana in 3, Fay in 4\n- Ana in 4, Fay in 5\n- Ana in 5, Fay in 6\n- Ana in 6, Fay in 7\n\nBut Eli is X+3 from Ana, so:\n- If Ana is in 1, Eli is in 4\n- Ana in 2, Eli in 5\n- Ana in 3, Eli in 6\n- Ana in 4, Eli in 7\n\nAna cannot be in 5 or 6 because then Eli would be in 8 or 9, which don't exist.\n\nNow, let's consider possible Ana positions:\n\n**Option 1: Ana in 1, Fay in 2, Eli in 4**\nFrom Clue 5: Gus is to the right of Eli. So Gus could be in 5,6, or 7.\nFrom Clue 2: Eli and Gus are not next to each other, so Gus cannot be in 5 (since Eli is in 4). So Gus is in 6 or 7.\n\nFrom Clue 4: Cai is to the left of Fay. Fay is in 2, so Cai must be in 1. But Ana is in 1, so this is a conflict. Hence, Ana cannot be in 1.\n\n**Option 2: Ana in 2, Fay in 3, Eli in 5**\nFrom Clue 5: Gus is to the right of Eli (5), so Gus in 6 or 7.\nFrom Clue 2: Eli and Gus not next to each other, so Gus cannot be in 6 (next to Eli in 5), so Gus in 7.\n\nFrom Clue 4: Cai is to the left of Fay (3). So Cai could be in 1 or 2. But Ana is in 2, so Cai in 1.\n\nNow, seats so far:\n1: Cai\n2: Ana\n3: Fay\n4: ?\n5: Eli\n6: ?\n7: Gus\n\nFrom Clue 3: Dev is to the right of Fay (3). So Dev is in 4,6, or 7. But 7 is Gus, so Dev in 4 or 6.\n\nFrom Clue 1: Exactly two between Ben and Dev. So possible pairs:\n- Ben in 1, Dev in 4: But 1 is Cai, so no.\n- Ben in 2, Dev in 5: 2 is Ana, 5 is Eli, so no.\n- Ben in 3, Dev in 6: 3 is Fay, so no.\n- Ben in 4, Dev in 7: 7 is Gus, so no.\nOther way: Dev first, then Ben:\n- Dev in 1, Ben in 4: 1 is Cai, so no.\n- Dev in 2, Ben in 5: 2 is Ana, 5 is Eli, no.\n- Dev in 3, Ben in 6: 3 is Fay, so no.\n- Dev in 4, Ben in 7: 7 is Gus, so no.\n\nHmm, no valid positions for Ben and Dev in this arrangement. So Ana cannot be in 2.\n\n**Option 3: Ana in 3, Fay in 4, Eli in 6**\nFrom Clue 5: Gus is to the right of Eli (6), so Gus in 7.\nFrom Clue 2: Eli and Gus not next to each other, but Eli is in 6, Gus in 7, so they are next to each other. This violates Clue 2. So Ana cannot be in 3.\n\n**Option 4: Ana in 4, Fay in 5, Eli in 7**\nFrom Clue 5: Gus is to the right of Eli (7), b"}
