y:     0000000110100110\n    - XOR:              11010010100001100 \u2192 108300 (Wait, let's compute carefully)\n    - Actually, 108192 ^ 422 = 108192 + 422 - 2*(108192 & 422)\n    - 108192 & 422 = 416\n    - So 108192 ^ 422 = 108192 + 422 - 832 = 107782 (This seems incorrect; better to compute directly)\n    - Let's do it bit by bit for the lower 16 bits (since & 0xFFFF at the end):\n      - 3381 in binary: 110100100101\n      - 3381 << 5: 11010010010100000 \u2192 108160 (but 3381 * 32 = 108192, so this is correct)\n      - 3381 >> 3: 110100100 \u2192 422 (correct)\n      - 108192 in hex: 0x1A6A0\n      - 422 in hex: 0x1A6\n      - 108192 ^ 422 = 0x1A6A0 ^ 0x1A6 = 0x1A706 = 108302\n  - 108302 ^ 321 = 108302 + 321 - 2*(108302 & 321)\n    - 108302 & 321 = 320\n    - So 108302 ^ 321 = 108302 + 321 - 640 = 107983\n- x = 107983 & 0xFFFF = 107983 - 4 * 65536 = 107983 - 262144 = negative? Wait, 107983 is less than 65536, so 107983 & 0xFFFF = 107983\n- Wait, 107983 is less than 65536? No, 65536 is 0x10000, so 107983 is greater. 107983 - 65536 = 42447\n- So x = 42447\n\nWait, let's recompute the XOR step more carefully:\n\nCompute 108192 ^ 422 ^ 321 step by step:\n\n1. 108192 ^ 422:\n   - 108192: 0b11010010010100000\n   - 422:    0b0000000110100110\n   - XOR:    0b1101001111110110 \u2192 108302 (correct)\n2. 108302 ^ 321:\n   - 108302: 0b1101001111110110\n   - 321:    0b0000000101000001\n   - XOR:    0b1101001010110111 \u2192 107983 (correct)\n3. 107983 & 0xFFFF:\n   - 107983 - 65536 = 42447\n\nSo x = 42447\n\n---\n\n**Iteration 4 (i=3, ch='e')**\n- x << 5 = 42447 << 5 = 1358304\n- x >> 3 = 42447 >> 3 = 5305\n- ord(ch) * (i + 1) = 101 * 4 = 404\n- x = (1358304 ^ 5305 ^ 404) & 0xFFFF\n  - 1358304 ^ 5305:\n    - 1358304: 0b101001011100000000000\n    - 5305:    0b00000000000101001011001\n    - XOR:     0b10100101110101001011001 \u2192 1358304 + 5305 - 2*(1358304 & 5305)\n    - 1358304 & 5305 = 5304\n    - So 1358304 ^ 5305 = 1358304 + 5305 - 10608 = 1353001\n  - 1353001 ^ 404:\n    - 1353001: 0b1010010101000011001\n    - 404:     0b0000000000000110010100\n    - XOR:     0b1010010101000101011001 \u2192 1353001 + 404 - 2*(1353001 & 404)\n    - 1353001 & 404 = 388\n    - So 1353001 ^ 404 = 1353001 + 404 - 776 = 1352629\n- x = 1352629 & 0xFFFF = 1352629 - 20 * 65536 = 1352629 - 1310720 = 41909\n\n---\n\n**Iteration 5 (i=4, ch='s')**\n- x << 5 = 41909 << 5 = 1341088\n- x >> 3 = 41909 >> 3 = 5238\n- ord(ch) * (i + 1) = 115 * 5 = 575\n- x = (1341088 ^ 5238 ^ 575) & 0xFFFF\n  - 1341088 ^ 5238:\n    - 1341088: 0b10100011101110000000\n    - 5238:    0b000000000001010001110110\n    - XOR:     0b101000111010110001110110 \u2192 1341088 + 5238 - 2*(1341088 & 5238)\n    - 1341088 & 5238 = 5232\n    - So 1341088 ^ 5238 = 1341088 + 5238 - 10464 = 1335862\n  - 1335862 ^ 575:\n    - 1335862: 0b10100010100111110110\n    - 575:     0b000000000000001000111111\n    - XOR:     0b101000101001110101011101 \u2192 1335862 + 575 - 2*(1335862 & 575)\n    - 1335862 & 575 = 574\n    - So 1335862 ^ 575 = 1335862 + 575 - 1148 = 1335289\n- x = 1335289 & 0xFFFF"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-013", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Most jobs finished by their deadlines", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 29251, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 4708, "reasoning_tokens": 4484, "input_tokens": 184, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 586, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 14881, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "6", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "6"}, "workflow_id": "0sfje58c8310-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfje53499c4-d029-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:10:09-0500", "answer": "Total processing time of all 8 jobs is \\(4+2+5+3+6+2+4+3 = 29\\) hours.\n\nIf 7 jobs were on time, even omitting the longest job (J5, 6 hours), the 7 on-time jobs would require at least \\(29 - 6 = 23\\) hours. But every job has deadline at most 20, so the last of those 7 jobs would finish after its deadline. Thus, at most 6 jobs can be on time.\n\nA schedule achieving 6 on-time jobs is:\n- J2: 0\u20132 (deadline 5)\n- J4: 2\u20135 (deadline 9)\n- J6: 5\u20137 (deadline 11)\n- J8: 7\u201310 (deadline 13)\n- J5: 10\u201316 (deadline 18)\n- J7: 16\u201320 (deadline 20)\n\nSo the maximum number of on-time jobs is 6.\n\nANSWER: 6"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "reasoning-013", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Most jobs finished by their deadlines", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": false, "score": 0.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 34983, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 3662, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 190, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 9365, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 0.0, "pass": false, "expected": "6", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "5"}, "workflow_id": "0sfje58c8312-d029-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfje53499c4-d029-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:10:15-0500", "answer": "Alright, let's tackle this problem step by step. We have eight jobs, each with a processing time and a deadline. The machine can handle one job at a time without interruption, starting at time 0. Our goal is to schedule these jobs in such a way that the maximum number of jobs finish on or before their deadlines.\n\n### Understanding the Problem\n\nFirst, let's list out the jobs with their processing times and deadlines:\n\n- J1: Processing time = 4, Deadline = 6\n- J2: Processing time = 2, Deadline = 5\n- J3: Processing time = 5, Deadline = 14\n- J4: Processing time = 3, Deadline = 9\n- J5: Processing time = 6, Deadline = 18\n- J6: Processing time = 2, Deadline = 11\n- J7: Processing time = 4, Deadline = 20\n- J8: Processing time = 3, Deadline = 13\n\nA job is \"on time\" if it finishes by its deadline. Late jobs still run but after the on-time jobs. We need to find the schedule that maximizes the number of on-time jobs.\n\n### Approach to Solve the Problem\n\nThis is a classic scheduling problem where we want to maximize the number of jobs completed by their deadlines on a single machine. The general approach is to use the **Earliest Deadline First (EDF)** algorithm, which schedules jobs in order of their deadlines, with the job having the earliest deadline being scheduled first.\n\nHowever, since we're trying to maximize the number of on-time jobs (not necessarily minimize lateness or something else), we might need to consider that sometimes scheduling a job with a slightly later deadline but shorter processing time could allow more jobs to finish on time.\n\nBut in most cases, EDF is a good starting point. Let's try scheduling the jobs in order of their deadlines and see how many we can get on time.\n\n### Applying Earliest Deadline First (EDF)\n\nLet's order the jobs by their deadlines:\n\n1. J2: deadline 5\n2. J1: deadline 6\n3. J4: deadline 9\n4. J6: deadline 11\n5. J8: deadline 13\n6. J3: deadline 14\n7. J5: deadline 18\n8. J7: deadline 20\n\nNow, let's schedule them in this order and see when each finishes:\n\n- Start at time 0.\n- Schedule J2: starts at 0, finishes at 0 + 2 = 2.\n  - Deadline is 5: 2 \u2264 5 \u2192 on time.\n- Schedule J1: starts at 2, finishes at 2 + 4 = 6.\n  - Deadline is 6: 6 \u2264 6 \u2192 on time.\n- Schedule J4: starts at 6, finishes at 6 + 3 = 9.\n  - Deadline is 9: 9 \u2264 9 \u2192 on time.\n- Schedule J6: starts at 9, finishes at 9 + 2 = 11.\n  - Deadline is 11: 11 \u2264 11 \u2192 on time.\n- Schedule J8: starts at 11, finishes at 11 + 3 = 14.\n  - Deadline is 13: 14 > 13 \u2192 late.\n  - Since it's late, we might want to see if we can rearrange to make it on time, but let's proceed for now.\n- Schedule J3: starts at 14, finishes at 14 + 5 = 19.\n  - Deadline is 14: 19 > 14 \u2192 late.\n- Schedule J5: starts at 19, finishes at 19 + 6 = 25.\n  - Deadline is 18: 25 > 18 \u2192 late.\n- Schedule J7: starts at 25, finishes at 25 + 4 = 29.\n  - Deadline is 20: 29 > 20 \u2192 late.\n\nSo, with EDF, we have J2, J1, J4, J6 on time (4 jobs), and the rest are late.\n\nBut we might be able to do better by choosing a different order where more jobs can finish on time. Maybe we can skip a job that would make others late and choose a different one.\n\n### Trying to Improve the Schedule\n\nLet's see if we can include J8 on time by adjusting the order. Maybe we can schedule J8 before J6.\n\nAlternative order after J4 (which finishes at 9):\n\nInstead of J6 next, let's try J8:\n\n- J8: starts at 9, finishes at 9 + 3 = 12.\n  - Deadline is 13: 12 \u2264 13 \u2192 on time.\n- Now, J6: starts at 12, finishes at 12 + 2 = 14.\n  - Deadline is 11: 14 > 11 \u2192 late.\n- Then J3: starts at 14, finishes at 14 + 5 = 19.\n  - Deadline is 14: 19 > 14 \u2192 late.\n- And so on...\n\nNow, we have J2, J1, J4, J8 on time (4 jobs), same as before, but J6 is late instead of J8.\n\nNot an improvement in count, but let's see if we can do better.\n\nWhat if we skip J6 entirely in the initial sequence and see:\n\nOriginal EDF sequence up to J4: J2, J1, J4 (finishes at 9).\n\nNow, instead of J6, let's try J8:\n\n- J8: starts at 9, finishes at 12 (on time).\n- Then J6: starts at 12, finishes "}
