{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "grounding-010", "category": "grounding", "title": "Vega-C flight VV30 payloads", "difficulty": "easy", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 8788, "turns": 1, "retries": 2, "usage": {"search_calls": 2, "output_tokens": 783, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 798, "search_tokens": 11486, "cached_input_tokens": null}, "answer_chars": 1146, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 1556, "detail": {"pass": true, "score": 1.0, "extracted": "Copernicus Sentinel-3C; FLEX (FLuorescence Explorer)", "expected": "FLEX (Fluorescence Explorer); Copernicus Sentinel-3C", "answer_line_found": true, "pattern_misses": [], "cited": true, "citation_count": 0}, "workflow_id": "0sfj6897d43b-d02c-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj64cf2092-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:28:47-0500", "answer": "Europe\u2019s Vega-C flight VV30 was a dual launch that lifted off from Europe\u2019s Spaceport in French Guiana on 14 September 2026 at 22:21 local time (15 September 01:21 UTC / 03:21 CEST). It carried two Earth-observation satellites, stacked with the Vespa secondary payload adapter, with Sentinel-3C deploying first and FLEX about an hour later:\n\n- **Copernicus Sentinel-3C** \u2014 the third satellite of the EU Copernicus Sentinel-3 mission (operated by EUMETSAT), for ocean, land, ice and atmosphere monitoring.\n- **FLEX (FLuorescence Explorer)** \u2014 ESA\u2019s Earth Explorer mission to monitor plant health via chlorophyll fluorescence.\n\nSources: ESA (\u201cVega-C takes FLEX and Sentinel-3C into orbit\u201d and \u201cVega-C flight VV30 with FLEX and Sentinel-3C\u201d) [search-result://HzC3B3NK, search-result://JFFLmFYp]; Avio press release, 30 July 2026 [search-result://iL78dUCf / search-result://KZCJa5oi]; CNES, \u201cVV30 : revivez le lancement de Vega-C avec les satellites Sentinel-3C et Flex\u201d [search-result://AYAjBxzn]; Spaceflight Now, 15 September 2026 [search-result://zLNB9rXZ / search-result://UEvD00ic].\n\nANSWER: Copernicus Sentinel-3C; FLEX (FLuorescence Explorer)", "rescored": "grounding citation rule v3 (URL, website or provider reference link)"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "grounding-010", "category": "grounding", "title": "Vega-C flight VV30 payloads", "difficulty": "easy", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 8863, "turns": 1, "retries": 2, "usage": {"search_calls": 3, "output_tokens": 680, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 798, "search_tokens": 17336, "cached_input_tokens": null}, "answer_chars": 1835, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"pass": true, "score": 1.0, "extracted": "Copernicus Sentinel-3C; FLEX (FLuorescence EXplorer)", "expected": "FLEX (Fluorescence Explorer); Copernicus Sentinel-3C", "answer_line_found": true, "pattern_misses": [], "cited": true, "citation_count": 5}, "workflow_id": "0sfj6897fb4c-d02c-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj64cf2092-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:28:47-0500", "answer": "Europe's Vega-C rocket launched two satellites on flight VV30 on September 15, 2026 (03:21 CEST / 01:21 UTC) from Europe's Spaceport in Kourou, French Guiana:\n\n1. **Copernicus Sentinel-3C** \u2014 the third satellite of the Sentinel-3 mission of the European Union's Copernicus programme, for ocean and atmosphere monitoring (managed by EUMETSAT).\n2. **FLEX (FLuorescence EXplorer)** \u2014 ESA's eighth Earth Explorer mission under the FutureEO programme, for monitoring plant health via chlorophyll fluorescence.\n\nThis was Vega-C's first dual launch, using the Vespa secondary payload adapter, with Sentinel-3C on top and FLEX below it inside the fairing. Both satellites were built by Thales Alenia Space and were deployed into a Sun-synchronous orbit at about 820 km altitude.\n\n**Sources:**\n- ESA \u2013 \"Vega-C flight VV30 with FLEX and Sentinel-3C\" (https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Images/2026/09/Vega-C_flight_VV30_with_FLEX_and_Sentinel-3C)\n- Avio press release \u2013 \"AVIO TO LAUNCH COPERNICUS SENTINEL-3C AND FLEX SATELLITES ON SEPTEMBER 14th 2026 ON VEGA C\" (https://www.avio.com/press-release/avio-to-launch-copernicus-sentinel-3-c-and-flex-satellites-on-september-14th-2026-on-vega-c)\n- GlobeNewswire / Genenta Science \u2013 \"S\u00f2phia High Tech Flight Hardware on Board VEGA-C VV30 as the European Launcher Carries Sentinel-3C and FLEX into Orbit\" (https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/09/15/3361705/0/en/...)\n- Spaceflight Now \u2013 \"Avio launches pair of climate monitoring European spacecraft to study plants, oceans and atmospheric changes\" (https://spaceflightnow.com/2026/09/15/avio-launches-pair-of-climate-monitoring-european-spacecraft-to-study-plants-oceans-and-atmospheric-changes/)\n- Wikipedia \u2013 \"FLEX (satellite)\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FLEX_(satellite))\n\nANSWER: Copernicus Sentinel-3C; FLEX (FLuorescence EXplorer)", "rescored": "grounding citation rule v3 (URL, website or provider reference link)"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "grounding-008", "category": "grounding", "title": "Kubernetes v1.37 release name and date", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 12820, "turns": 1, "retries": 2, "usage": {"search_calls": 4, "output_tokens": 517, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 804, "search_tokens": 17719, "cached_input_tokens": null}, "answer_chars": 1279, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"pass": true, "score": 1.0, "extracted": "Garhwal; 2026-08-26", "expected": "Garhwal; 2026-08-26", "answer_line_found": true, "pattern_misses": [], "cited": true, "citation_count": 3}, "workflow_id": "0sfj666291e6-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj64cf2094-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:28:50-0500", "answer": "Based on my web search and verification from official sources:\n\n**Release name:** Kubernetes v1.37 is codenamed **\"Garhwal\"** (\u0917\u0922\u093c\u0935\u093e\u0932, pronounced *ga\u1e5bhv\u0101l*), named after the Himalayan region of Uttarakhand, India. This is confirmed by the official Kubernetes blog post titled \"Kubernetes v1.37: Garhwal\" [yZW0ADIG, ax5xqvh2].\n\n**Release date:** Kubernetes v1.37.0 was released on **August 26, 2026**. This is confirmed by:\n- The official Kubernetes blog post dated \"Wednesday, August 26, 2026\" [yZW0ADIG, ax5xqvh2]\n- The GitHub release page for v1.37.0 showing \"k8s-release-robot released this 26 Aug\" with source code assets timestamped `2026-08-26T10:44:20Z` [2wZ3qBWo, 1jyrzpu5]\n- The kubernetes/sig-release repository schedule showing \"Wednesday 26th August 2026: Week 15 \u2014 Kubernetes v1.37.0 released\" [0jEOklso]\n\n**Sources cited:**\n- [yZW0ADIG, ax5xqvh2] Official Kubernetes Blog: \"Kubernetes v1.37: Garhwal\" \u2014 https://kubernetes.io/blog/2026/08/26/kubernetes-v1-37-release/\n- [2wZ3qBWo, 1jyrzpu5] GitHub: kubernetes/kubernetes Release v1.37.0 \u2014 https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/releases/tag/v1.37.0\n- [0jEOklso] kubernetes/sig-release: release-1.37 schedule \u2014 https://github.com/kubernetes/sig-release/tree/master/releases/release-1.37\n\nANSWER: Garhwal; 2026-08-26", "rescored": "grounding citation rule v3 (URL, website or provider reference link)"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "grounding-007", "category": "grounding", "title": "Python 3.15.0 release date and release manager", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 16297, "turns": 1, "retries": 2, "usage": {"search_calls": 8, "output_tokens": 796, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 814, "search_tokens": 27868, "cached_input_tokens": null}, "answer_chars": 1869, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"pass": true, "score": 1.0, "extracted": "2026-10-09; Hugo van Kemenade", "expected": "2026-10-09; Hugo van Kemenade", "answer_line_found": true, "pattern_misses": [], "cited": true, "citation_count": 5}, "workflow_id": "0sfj666a89f1-d02c-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj64cef97e-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:28:51-0500", "answer": "Based on my research, here are the findings:\n\n**Final Python 3.15.0 release date:** The official Python.org release page for Python 3.15.0 states \"Release date: Oct. 9, 2026\" and identifies it as \"the stable release of Python 3.15.0\" [SE6zKSNn]. This is confirmed by PEP 790 (Python 3.15 Release Schedule), which lists \"3.15.0 final: Friday, 2026-10-09\" under the \"Actual\" schedule [pFvJ3rN3]. The release manager's own blog post \"Releasing Python 3.15.0\" also documents the release day activities on Friday, October 9, 2026, ending with \"Out now: Python 3.15.0 final!\" [uNUO0yyx].\n\nNote: The release was originally planned for October 1, 2026, but was postponed by one week due to last-minute lazy-import release blockers, which led to an unplanned third release candidate (3.15.0rc3) on October 2, 2026 [ETTHrKP9, uNUO0yyx].\n\n**Release manager for Python 3.15:** PEP 790 explicitly lists \"3.15 release manager: Hugo van Kemenade\" [pFvJ3rN3]. This is confirmed by the official Python Insider blog announcement signed \"Your release team, Hugo van Kemenade, Ned Deily, Steve Dower\" [nk8j5oLe], and by the python.org release page which thanks \"the Sovereign Tech Agency for supporting Hugo van Kemenade's work as release manager for Python 3.14 and 3.15\" [SE6zKSNn].\n\n**Sources cited:**\n- [SE6zKSNn] Python.org \u2013 Python Release Python 3.15.0: https://www.python.org/downloads/release/python-3150/\n- [pFvJ3rN3] PEP 790 \u2013 Python 3.15 Release Schedule: https://peps.python.org/pep-0790/\n- [nk8j5oLe] Python Insider \u2013 Python 3.15.0 (final) is here!: https://blog.python.org/2026/10/python-3150-final-is-here/\n- [uNUO0yyx] Hugo van Kemenade \u2013 Releasing Python 3.15.0: https://hugovk.dev/blog/2026/releasing-python-3-15-0/\n- [ETTHrKP9] Python.org \u2013 Python Release Python 3.15.0rc3: https://www.python.org/downloads/release/python-3150rc3/\n\nANSWER: 2026-10-09; Hugo van Kemenade", "rescored": "grounding citation rule v3 (URL, website or provider reference link)"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "grounding-007", "category": "grounding", "title": "Python 3.15.0 release date and release manager", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 17280, "turns": 1, "retries": 2, "usage": {"search_calls": 4, "output_tokens": 775, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 814, "search_tokens": 12153, "cached_input_tokens": null}, "answer_chars": 1212, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 1043, "detail": {"pass": true, "score": 1.0, "extracted": "2026-10-09; Hugo van Kemenade", "expected": "2026-10-09; Hugo van Kemenade", "answer_line_found": true, "pattern_misses": [], "cited": true, "citation_count": 3}, "workflow_id": "0sfj666a89f3-d02c-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj64cef97e-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:28:53-0500", "answer": "**Findings:**\n\n**Release date:** The official python.org release page for Python 3.15.0 states \"Release date: Oct. 9, 2026\" [1]. This is corroborated by PEP 790 (Python 3.15 Release Schedule), which lists \"3.15.0 final: Friday, 2026-10-09\" under its \"Actual\" schedule [2]. Note that the final release was originally scheduled for October 1, 2026, but was pushed back one week after an unplanned third release candidate (3.15.0rc3) was issued on October 2, 2026 to fix late lazy-import release blockers [2][3].\n\n**Release manager:** PEP 790 explicitly lists \"3.15 release manager: Hugo van Kemenade\" [2]. The python.org 3.15.0 release page also thanks \"the Sovereign Tech Agency for supporting Hugo van Kemenade's work as release manager for Python 3.14 and 3.15\" [1], and his personal blog documents the release process for Python 3.15.0 [3].\n\n**Sources:**\n- [1] Python.org \u2014 \"Python Release Python 3.15.0\": https://www.python.org/downloads/release/python-3150/\n- [2] PEP 790 \u2013 Python 3.15 Release Schedule: https://peps.python.org/pep-0790/\n- [3] Hugo van Kemenade \u2014 \"Releasing Python 3.15.0\" (release manager's blog): https://hugovk.dev/blog/2026/releasing-python-3-15-0/\n\nANSWER: 2026-10-09; Hugo van Kemenade", "rescored": "grounding citation rule v3 (URL, website or provider reference link)"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "grounding-005", "category": "grounding", "title": "2026 US Open women's singles final score", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 14340, "turns": 1, "retries": 2, "usage": {"search_calls": 6, "output_tokens": 650, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 809, "search_tokens": 24875, "cached_input_tokens": null}, "answer_chars": 1405, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"pass": true, "score": 1.0, "extracted": "Elena Rybakina def. Aryna Sabalenka 6\u20134, 5\u20137, 6\u20132", "expected": "Elena Rybakina def. Aryna Sabalenka 6-4, 5-7, 6-2", "answer_line_found": true, "pattern_misses": [], "cited": true, "citation_count": 0}, "workflow_id": "0sfj6853e382-d02c-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj64cef97d-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:28:53-0500", "answer": "Based on my web searches, I found consistent reporting from multiple sources about the 2026 US Open women's singles final.\n\n**Elena Rybakina** of Kazakhstan won the 2026 US Open women's singles title, defeating two-time defending champion **Aryna Sabalenka** of Belarus in the final on Saturday, September 12, 2026, at Arthur Ashe Stadium in New York. The final score was **6\u20134, 5\u20137, 6\u20132**.\n\nThis was Rybakina's first US Open title and her third Grand Slam singles title overall (her second major of 2026, having also beaten Sabalenka in the Australian Open final earlier that year). The victory also ended Sabalenka's 99-week reign as world No. 1, with Rybakina ascending to the top ranking.\n\n**Sources cited:**\n- US Open official website: \"Rybakina defeats two-time defending champion Sabalenka to win 2026 US Open\" (usopen.org) and official draw PDF showing \"Champion: RYBAKINA, Elena KAZ 6-4 | 5-7 | 6-2\"\n- BBC Sport: \"US Open 2026 results: Elena Rybakina beats Aryna Sabalenka to win New York title\" (12 September 2026)\n- Associated Press (AP News): \"Elena Rybakina beats Aryna Sabalenka to win the US Open title\"\n- WTA official website: match record showing Sabalenka vs. Rybakina final, 4-6, 7-5, 2-6\n- USA Today: \"US Open women's final: Elena Rybakina wins to deny Aryna Sabalenka three-peat\"\n- Wikipedia: \"2026 US Open \u2013 Women's singles\"\n\nANSWER: Elena Rybakina def. Aryna Sabalenka 6\u20134, 5\u20137, 6\u20132", "rescored": "grounding citation rule v3 (URL, website or provider reference link)"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "grounding-008", "category": "grounding", "title": "Kubernetes v1.37 release name and date", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 15804, "turns": 1, "retries": 2, "usage": {"search_calls": 4, "output_tokens": 951, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 804, "search_tokens": 18409, "cached_input_tokens": null}, "answer_chars": 1532, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 1452, "detail": {"pass": true, "score": 1.0, "extracted": "Garhwal; 2026-08-26", "expected": "Garhwal; 2026-08-26", "answer_line_found": true, "pattern_misses": [], "cited": true, "citation_count": 3}, "workflow_id": "0sfj666291e5-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj64cf2094-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:28:53-0500", "answer": "I'll search the web for the Kubernetes v1.37 release details.The search results are consistent. Let me verify directly against the official Kubernetes release blog and the GitHub release page.I have confirmed the details from the official Kubernetes sources.\n\n**Release name:** Kubernetes v1.37 is named **Garhwal** (\u0917\u0922\u093c\u0935\u093e\u0932, pronounced *ga\u1e5bhv\u0101l*), after the Himalayan region of Uttarakhand, India. The official release announcement is titled \"Kubernetes v1.37: Garhwal,\" and the SIG Release page for the 1.37 cycle states: \"Kubernetes v1.37 is named Garhwal (\u0917\u0922\u093c\u0935\u093e\u0932, pronounced ga\u1e5bhv\u0101l), after the Himalayan region of Uttarakhand, India.\"\n\n**Release date of v1.37.0:** **Wednesday, August 26, 2026**. This is confirmed by three independent sources:\n- The official Kubernetes blog post \"Kubernetes v1.37: Garhwal\" is dated Wednesday, August 26, 2026.\n- The GitHub release page for `v1.37.0` shows it was published by k8s-release-robot on 26 Aug (2026), with release assets timestamped 2026-08-26T10:44:20Z.\n- The SIG Release schedule for the 1.37 cycle lists \"Wednesday 26th August 2026: Week 15 \u2014 Kubernetes v1.37.0 released.\"\n\n**Sources cited:**\n1. Kubernetes Blog \u2014 \"Kubernetes v1.37: Garhwal\" (https://kubernetes.io/blog/2026/08/26/kubernetes-v1-37-release/)\n2. GitHub \u2014 kubernetes/kubernetes Release v1.37.0 (https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/releases/tag/v1.37.0)\n3. kubernetes/sig-release \u2014 release-1.37 schedule (https://github.com/kubernetes/sig-release/tree/master/releases/release-1.37)\n\nANSWER: Garhwal; 2026-08-26", "rescored": "grounding citation rule v3 (URL, website or provider reference link)"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "grounding-012", "category": "grounding", "title": "Mistral Large 4 announcement and size", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 21000, "turns": 1, "retries": 2, "usage": {"search_calls": 8, "output_tokens": 579, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 813, "search_tokens": 38828, "cached_input_tokens": null}, "answer_chars": 1529, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"pass": true, "score": 1.0, "extracted": "2026-10-06; 1.05 trillion (1T) total; 52 billion active", "expected": "2026-10-06; 1 trillion total; 52 billion active", "answer_line_found": true, "pattern_misses": [], "cited": true, "citation_count": 3}, "workflow_id": "0sfj64f66a38-d02c-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj64cf2091-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:28:55-0500", "answer": "Based on my research, I can now provide you with the answer.\n\n**Announcement Date:** Mistral AI announced Mistral Large 4 on **October 6, 2026**, as a public preview through the Mistral Studio API. The official announcement post is titled \"Introducing Mistral Large 4\" and is dated October 6, 2026 on Mistral's website.\n\n**Parameter Counts:** According to Mistral's official announcement and documentation:\n- **Total parameters:** 1 trillion (more precisely documented as **1.05 trillion** in Mistral's technical documentation)\n- **Active parameters:** **52 billion** (as stated in both the current announcement and the official model documentation)\n\nNote: There was some initial confusion in early coverage, with some reports citing 49 billion active parameters. However, Mistral's official documentation clarifies this distinction: 49 billion refers to active parameters in the MoE experts only, while 52 billion is the inclusive count that also includes embeddings and output layers. Mistral's current official announcement and model card both use the **52 billion** figure.\n\n**Sources:**\n1. Mistral AI official announcement: \"Introducing Mistral Large 4\" (October 6, 2026) \u2014 https://mistral.ai/news/mistral-large-4/\n2. Mistral AI official documentation: \"Mistral Large 4 - Mistral Docs\" \u2014 https://docs.mistral.ai/models/mistral-large-4\n3. Kingy AI analysis clarifying the 49B vs 52B distinction \u2014 https://kingy.ai/blog/mistral-large-4-specs-benchmarks-pricing/\n\nANSWER: 2026-10-06; 1.05 trillion (1T) total; 52 billion active", "rescored": "grounding citation rule v3 (URL, website or provider reference link)"}
