 false\n   - `published_at`: 2026-09-25T04:58:15Z (before the Oct 20, 2026 cutoff)\n\nBoth sources agree: as of October 20, 2026, the v3.33.0 line had only reached release candidates (latest rc13 on Oct 14, 2026), so the most recent **stable** release was v3.32.5, published September 25, 2026.\n\n**Sources:**\n- GitHub Releases page: https://github.com/conductor-oss/conductor/releases\n- GitHub Releases API (latest stable release): https://api.github.com/repos/conductor-oss/conductor/releases/latest\n\nANSWER: v3.32.5", "rescored": "grounding citation rule v3 (URL, website or provider reference link)"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "grounding-006", "category": "grounding", "title": "2026 Asian Games medal table leader", "difficulty": "easy", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 20331, "turns": 1, "retries": 2, "usage": {"search_calls": 4, "output_tokens": 1793, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 814, "search_tokens": 27178, "cached_input_tokens": null}, "answer_chars": 1658, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 4343, "detail": {"pass": true, "score": 1.0, "extracted": "China; 169", "expected": "China; 169", "answer_line_found": true, "pattern_misses": [], "cited": true, "citation_count": 0}, "workflow_id": "0sfj66979743-d02c-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj64cf2090-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:28:58-0500", "answer": "**China** finished top of the medal table at the 20th Asian Games in Aichi\u2013Nagoya, Japan (held 19 September \u2013 4 October 2026), winning **169 gold medals** (along with 89 silver and 83 bronze, for 341 medals in total). This marked China's 12th consecutive time topping the Asian Games medal table.\n\nThis is confirmed by multiple independent sources:\n\n- **Wikipedia \u2013 2026 Asian Games medal table**: China ranked 1st with 169 gold, 89 silver, 83 bronze (341 total) [search-result://K2XApIk0]\n- **Xinhua (official Chinese state news agency)**: \"China topping the gold medal table for the 12th consecutive edition, and finishing with 169 gold, 89 silver and 83 bronze medals\" [search-result://t4RF1l2o]\n- **KhelNow**: \"China topped the final medal standings with 169 gold, 89 silver and 83 bronze medals, for a total of 341 medals\" [search-result://zArv5dgG]\n- **Times of India**: \"1. China: 169 Gold | 89 Silver | 83 Bronze | Total: 341\" [search-result://eYRFR1tD]\n- **Indian Express**: \"China conquered the Asian Games in Aichi-Nagoya, claiming a whopping 341 medals, 169 of them gold\" [search-result://TRtwE03K]\n- **Sports League**: \"China topped the table for the 12th Games in a row with 169 gold and 341 medals in total\" [search-result://aOONXhCW]\n- **The Mainichi (Kyodo)**: Host Japan won a record 83 golds, while \"China once again underlined its dominance by taking more than twice as many golds as the host nation\" \u2014 consistent with 169 golds [search-result://LIkbABsZ]\n\nFor context, host nation Japan finished second with 83 golds (269 total), South Korea third with 39 golds (150 total), and India fourth with 21 golds (85 total).\n\nANSWER: China; 169", "rescored": "grounding citation rule v3 (URL, website or provider reference link)"}
{"run_id": "full-r2", "item_id": "grounding-005", "category": "grounding", "title": "2026 US Open women's singles final score", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 24705, "turns": 1, "retries": 2, "usage": {"search_calls": 2, "output_tokens": 909, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 809, "search_tokens": 12156, "cached_input_tokens": null}, "answer_chars": 1375, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 1480, "detail": {"pass": true, "score": 1.0, "extracted": "Elena Rybakina def. Aryna Sabalenka 6-4, 5-7, 6-2", "expected": "Elena Rybakina def. Aryna Sabalenka 6-4, 5-7, 6-2", "answer_line_found": true, "pattern_misses": [], "cited": true, "citation_count": 4}, "workflow_id": "0sfj6853e37f-d02c-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj64cef97d-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:29:03-0500", "answer": "I'll search for the 2026 US Open women's singles final results.**Elena Rybakina** won the 2026 US Open women's singles title, defeating two-time defending champion **Aryna Sabalenka** in the final, played Saturday, September 12, 2026, at Arthur Ashe Stadium. The score was **6\u20134, 5\u20137, 6\u20132**.\n\nThe win gave Rybakina (Kazakhstan) her third Grand Slam title overall and her second major of 2026 (she also won the Australian Open), and it lifted her to world No. 1. It was also the first US Open final between the top two seeds since 2013. Maria Sharapova presented Rybakina with the trophy, a tribute to Sharapova's own 2006 US Open victory.\n\nSources:\n- Wikipedia, \"2026 US Open \u2013 Women's singles\" \u2014 https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_US_Open_%E2%80%93_Women's_singles\n- US Open official site, \"Rybakina defeats two-time defending champion Sabalenka to win 2026 US Open\" (Sept. 12, 2026) \u2014 https://www.usopen.org/en_US/news/articles/2026-09-12/rybakina_defeats_two-time_defending_champion_sabalenka_to_win_2026_us_open.html\n- US Open official 2026 Women's Singles draw (champion: RYBAKINA, Elena KAZ \u2014 6-4 | 5-7 | 6-2) \u2014 https://www.usopen.org/en_US/scores/draws/2026_WS_draw.pdf\n- BBC Sport, \"US Open 2026 final LIVE: Elena Rybakina beats Aryna Sabalenka for title\" \u2014 https://www.bbc.com/sport/tennis/live/ckgd2jp4pxvt\n\nANSWER: Elena Rybakina def. Aryna Sabalenka 6-4, 5-7, 6-2", "rescored": "grounding citation rule v3 (URL, website or provider reference link)"}
